Perfect! Now I can see the issue clearly. In the AttributeKeyPair constructor at line 422-438:

```java
public AttributeKeyPair(String key, String value, boolean trimQuoted) {
    Validate.notEmpty(key);
    Validate.notEmpty(value);

    this.key = normalize(key);
    boolean quoted = value.startsWith("'") && value.endsWith("'")
        || value.startsWith("\"") && value.endsWith("\"");
    if (quoted)
        value = value.substring(1, value.length() - 1);

    // normalize value based on whether it was quoted and trimQuoted flag
    // keeps whitespace for attribute val starting or ending, when quoted
    if (trimQuoted || !quoted)
        this.value = normalize(value); // lowercase and trims
    else
        this.value = lowerCase(value); // only lowercase
}
```

The logic here is:
- If trimQuoted is true OR the value was unquoted, then call `normalize(value)` which trims and lowercases
- Otherwise (if quoted and trimQuoted is false), just lowercase

But the issue is that there's a validation check `Validate.notEmpty(value)` that happens BEFORE the quotes are stripped. So if someone writes `[data='']`, the value passed is `''` (which is 2 characters), so it passes validation. But then after stripping quotes, it becomes empty.

Also, I need to check what the subclasses are passing for the `trimQuoted` parameter. Let me look at the constructors of the attribute evaluators again.

From the earlier semantic search, I saw:
- AttributeWithValue (line 269): calls `super(key, value)` - no third parameter
- AttributeWithValueStarting (line 317): calls `super(key, value, false)`


- AttributeWithValueEnding (line 340): calls `super(key, value, false)`
- AttributeWithValueContaining (line 363): doesn't show explicit super call

So AttributeWithValue doesn't have an explicit second parameter, which means there's likely a two-parameter constructor overload. I need to examine the full AttributeKeyPair class definition and all its subclasses to understand the complete constructor hierarchy.

